    'body' => <<<'HTML'
<p><strong>How effective is an under the desk elliptical?</strong> Measured in energy use, it roughly doubles what you burn while sitting at a moderate pace, and adds about 50 percent at a light one. That puts it in the same range as a slow walk, but below brisk walking and well below a full-size elliptical at the gym.</p>
<p>Those numbers sound small per hour. The reason under desk ellipticals are effective anyway is that you can keep them going for hours while you work, which you cannot do with a walk or a gym session.</p>
<p>Below are the MET values, calories per hour for different body weights, a weekly picture, and the specific changes that get more out of every session.</p>

<h2>What a MET Is (and Why It Matters Here)</h2>
<p>A MET (metabolic equivalent of task) is a way of comparing activities by how much energy they use. One MET is roughly your energy use at complete rest. An activity rated 3 METs uses about three times that.</p>
<p>The values below come from the Compendium of Physical Activities, the standard reference researchers use. They are averages, so your own numbers will vary with fitness, body size and how hard you push. Public health guidelines classify 3.0 to 5.9 METs as moderate intensity and anything below 3.0 as light.</p>

<h2>How Effective Is an Under the Desk Elliptical Compared With Other Activities?</h2>
<p>This table compares common activities for a 70 kg (154 lb) adult. Calories are total burn, including what your body would use anyway.</p>
<div class="table-wrap"><table>
<thead><tr><th>Activity</th><th>Approx. METs</th><th>Calories per hour (70 kg)</th><th>Intensity</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Sitting quietly</td><td>1.3</td><td>~96</td><td>Sedentary</td></tr>
<tr><td>Typing, desk work</td><td>1.5</td><td>~110</td><td>Sedentary</td></tr>
<tr><td>Seated pedaling, light</td><td>2.0</td><td>~147</td><td>Light</td></tr>
<tr><td>Seated pedaling, moderate</td><td>2.8</td><td>~206</td><td>Light (upper end)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Walking, slow (about 2 mph)</td><td>2.8</td><td>~206</td><td>Light</td></tr>
<tr><td>Walking, 3 mph</td><td>3.5</td><td>~257</td><td>Moderate</td></tr>
<tr><td>Walking, brisk (about 3.5 mph)</td><td>4.3</td><td>~316</td><td>Moderate</td></tr>
<tr><td>Full-size elliptical, moderate effort</td><td>5.0</td><td>~368</td><td>Moderate</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<p>The takeaway: a moderate under desk pace matches a slow walk. You would need to work noticeably harder than most people do while typing to reach the 3.0 MET line for moderate intensity. That is possible during short, focused intervals with higher resistance.</p>
<div class="callout"><p><strong>Calorie formula:</strong> calories per minute = METs × 3.5 × body weight in kg ÷ 200. Multiply by 60 for an hour. All figures here use that formula.</p></div>

<h2>Calories per Hour by Body Weight</h2>
<p>Heavier bodies use more energy to move, so the same pace burns more. Here are total calories per hour at light and moderate seated pedaling:</p>
<div class="table-wrap"><table>
<thead><tr><th>Body weight</th><th>Light pace (2.0 METs)</th><th>Moderate pace (2.8 METs)</th><th>Extra over sitting (moderate)</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>60 kg (132 lb)</td><td>~126</td><td>~176</td><td>~95</td></tr>
<tr><td>70 kg (154 lb)</td><td>~147</td><td>~206</td><td>~110</td></tr>
<tr><td>80 kg (176 lb)</td><td>~168</td><td>~235</td><td>~126</td></tr>
<tr><td>100 kg (220 lb)</td><td>~210</td><td>~294</td><td>~158</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<p>The "extra over sitting" column is the one that matters for weight goals, because you would burn the sitting calories anyway. Enter your own weight, time and intensity here:</p>
[[tool:calories]]
<p>For more on what the display on your machine is telling you, see <a href="/does-under-desk-elliptical-burn-calories/">does an under desk elliptical burn calories</a>.</p>

<h2>Are Under Desk Ellipticals Effective Over a Week?</h2>
<p>Single sessions look modest. Weekly totals are where under desk ellipticals become effective. For a 70 kg adult, using the extra-over-sitting figures:</p>
<div class="table-wrap"><table>
<thead><tr><th>Daily use (5 days a week)</th><th>Light pace</th><th>Moderate pace</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>30 minutes a day</td><td>~130 extra calories/week</td><td>~275 extra calories/week</td></tr>
<tr><td>1 hour a day</td><td>~255 extra calories/week</td><td>~550 extra calories/week</td></tr>
<tr><td>2 hours a day</td><td>~510 extra calories/week</td><td>~1,100 extra calories/week</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<p>Two hours a day at a moderate pace adds up to roughly the energy of a few brisk walks each week, without taking any time out of your schedule. Whether that changes your weight depends on what you eat; our guide on <a href="/can-you-lose-weight-with-under-desk-elliptical/">losing weight with an under desk elliptical</a> covers the diet side.</p>

<h2>A Sample Workday, by the Numbers</h2>
<p>Here is how a realistic day might look for a 70 kg (154 lb) remote worker who pedals in blocks rather than nonstop:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Morning email (30 minutes, light):</strong> about 25 extra calories over sitting.</li>
<li><strong>Two video calls (60 minutes, moderate):</strong> about 110 extra calories.</li>
<li><strong>Afternoon reading (30 minutes, moderate):</strong> about 55 extra calories.</li>
<li><strong>One interval block (10 minutes, hard):</strong> roughly 25 to 35 extra calories, depending on how hard you push.</li>
</ul>
<p>That is around 220 extra calories in a little over two hours of pedaling, with no time taken out of the workday. Repeat it on five days and you are past 1,000 extra calories a week.</p>

<h2>Who Gets the Most Out of It</h2>
<p>The same machine can be very effective for one person and barely noticeable for another. It tends to make the biggest difference if you:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Sit for most of the day.</strong> The less you move now, the larger the relative change from adding pedaling.</li>
<li><strong>Weigh more.</strong> As the tables show, heavier bodies burn more at the same pace.</li>
<li><strong>Are new to exercise or older.</strong> For a low-fitness starting point, a brisk seated pace is a real effort and can build leg endurance over time.</li>
<li><strong>Already work out.</strong> For fit people, it adds daily energy use but will not move fitness much on its own. The value is in the extra volume, not the intensity.</li>
</ul>

<h2>How It Compares With a Desk Bike, Walking Pad and Walking</h2>
<p>Under desk bikes cover the same MET range as seated ellipticals, though some offer more resistance at the top end. Walking pads used at a standing desk usually sit around 2.0 to 3.5 METs depending on speed, and they add the benefits of being on your feet, but they need a standing desk and are harder to combine with typing. Regular outdoor walking at a brisk pace beats all of them per minute. For a direct comparison, read <a href="/is-under-desk-elliptical-as-good-as-walking/">is an under desk elliptical as good as walking</a>.</p>

<h2>Are Seated Ellipticals Effective Beyond Calories?</h2>
<p>Calories are the easiest thing to measure, but not the only effect. Seated pedaling keeps your thigh, hip and calf muscles contracting, keeps your knees and ankles moving, and breaks up long sedentary stretches, which health guidelines encourage on their own. These effects are harder to put numbers on, and we will not invent figures for them. For a goal-by-goal view, including circulation, stiffness and focus, see <a href="/does-under-desk-elliptical-work/">under desk elliptical results by goal</a>.</p>

<h2>Why the Numbers on Your Display May Differ</h2>
<p>Many machines show calories on their screen. Treat those as rough estimates. Most displays do not know your weight, and some count total calories while others try to estimate only the active portion. Resistance settings and stride length also vary between models, so 30 minutes on one machine may not match 30 minutes on another.</p>
<p>Use the display to compare your own sessions over time rather than as an exact figure. If you want a consistent number, log minutes and use the calculator above.</p>

<h2>How to Make an Under Desk Elliptical More Effective</h2>
<p>You cannot change your weight overnight, but you can change intensity and time. These are the levers, roughly in order of impact.</p>
<h3>1. Raise resistance during non-typing tasks</h3>
<p>Calls, videos, reading and thinking time do not need your hands to be steady. Turn resistance up for those and down for detailed typing. This one habit moves many people from 2.0 toward 2.8 METs for a good chunk of the day.</p>
<h3>2. Add short intervals</h3>
<p>Try 1 minute of hard pedaling followed by 2 minutes easy, repeated five times, once or twice a day. Hard bursts can push you briefly into moderate intensity, which a steady crawl never will.</p>
<h3>3. Increase total time</h3>
<p>Going from 30 minutes to 2 hours a day quadruples the weekly effect. Split it into blocks of 15 to 20 minutes to make it sustainable. A <a href="/tools/desk-pedal-timer/">desk pedal timer</a> helps.</p>
<h3>4. Choose a machine with real resistance</h3>
<p>A machine that maxes out at an easy level caps how effective it can be. Magnetic models with a wide range, like the [[amazon:deskcycle-ellipse|DeskCycle Ellipse]], leave room to progress. Our guide to the <a href="/under-desk-elliptical-with-resistance/">under desk elliptical with resistance</a> lists more.</p>
<h3>5. Pedal actively, even on a motorized model</h3>
<p>Passive mode on a motorized machine barely raises your burn above sitting. If you own one, pedal along with the motor or switch to manual mode for part of each session. We compare types in <a href="/do-under-desk-elliptical-machines/">manual vs motorized under desk elliptical machines</a>.</p>

<h2>Are Under Desk Elliptical Machines Effective? Models With Room to Grow</h2>
<p>Effectiveness depends heavily on resistance range and how comfortable the machine is to use for hours. Based on specs and owner reviews, these suit people who want to push their numbers up:</p>
